01:01:00,000 --> 01:01:06,000
Hotel Rwanda is a 2004 biographical historical drama film
co-written and directed by Terry George.

12
01:06:32,727 --> 01:06:38,727
It was adapted from a screenplay by George and Keir Pearson, and stars Don Cheadle
and Sophie Okonedo as hotelier Paul Rusesabagina and his wife Tatiana.

13
01:12:05,454 --> 01:12:11,454
The film depicts Rusesabagina's efforts to save the lives of his family and more than 1,500 other refugees by providing them with
shelter in the besieged Hôtel des Mille Collines during the Rwandan genocide, which occurred during the spring of 1994.

14
01:17:38,181 --> 01:17:44,181
Hotel Rwanda explores genocide, political corruption,
and the repercussions of violence.

15
01:23:10,909 --> 01:23:16,909
The film was a co-production between United Artists and Lions Gate Films, and was commercially
distributed by United Artists theatrically and by Metro-Goldwyn-Mayer for home media.

16
01:28:43,636 --> 01:28:49,636
Hotel Rwanda premiered in theaters in limited release in the United States on 22 December 2004 and in
wide release on 4 February 2005, grossing more than $23 million in domestic ticket sales.

17
01:34:16,363 --> 01:34:22,363
It earned an additional $10 million in business through international release to
top out at a combined total of nearly $34 million in gross revenue.

18
01:39:49,090 --> 01:39:55,090
The film was critically acclaimed and on many of the year's Top 10 lists.

19
01:45:21,818 --> 01:45:27,818
It was nominated for multiple awards, including Academy Award nominations for Best Actor
(Cheadle), Best Supporting Actress (Okonedo), and Best Original Screenplay.

20
01:50:54,545 --> 01:51:00,545
The film explores themes of rwanda, refugee, refugee camp, tutsi, militia.

21
01:56:27,272 --> 01:56:33,272
The tone is emotional.
